{"url": "https://livrepository.liverpool.ac.uk/3002404/1/FuArisoyShackletonUmutlu2016.pdf", "title": "Option-Implied Volatility Measures and Stock Return Predictability", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Using firm-level option and stock data, we examine the predictive ability of option-implied volatility measures proposed by previous studies and recommend the best measure using up to-date data. Portfolio level analysis implies significant non-zero risk-adjusted returns on arbitrage portfolios formed on the call-put implied volatility spread, implied volatility skew, and realized-implied volatility spread. Firm-level cross-sectional regressions show that, the implied volatility skew has the most significant predictive power over various investment horizons. The predictive power persists before and after the 2008 Global Financial Crisis.\n...\nTo compare the predictive power of option-implied volatility measures, we first form quintile portfolios sorted with respect to six option-implied volatility measures: the call-put implied volatility spread (CPIV), the implied volatility skew (IVSKEW ), the “above-minus below” (AMB), the “out-minus-at” of calls (COMA), the “out-minus-at” of puts (POMA), and the realized-implied volatility spread (RVIV ). Then, we construct zero-cost arbitrage portfolios by taking a long position in portfolios with the highest implied volatility measure and a short position in portfolios with the lowest implied volatility measure. The arbitrage\n...\nOur study contributes to the literature in several aspects. First, this study compares the predictive ability of six different implied volatility measures. To the best of our knowledge, this is the most comprehen", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 398602, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.5 %���� 1 0 obj >>> endobj 2 0 obj > endobj 3 0 obj >/ProcSet[/PDF/Text/ImageB/ImageC/ImageI] >>/MediaBox[ 0 0 595.32 841.92] /Contents 4 0 R/Group >/Tabs/S/StructParents 0>> endobj 4 0 obj > stream x��[�r��\u0011��*�\u0003~bm.�9��]�袝DJ�&U���\u0007� �\b�`vA*� ���\u0016��\u0019 �ŀ+ǎ��c�����\u0018��宮��E }���˺.\u0016��2�xz������������\u0014u�ݜ^ ��8 \u0013\u0017\u0011�I*=��NX.���D8\u0015�MS���L�\u001b%�7�{#_��y���� ;���[�\u0001�\\\u000fi�4�\\�D ,�-��l.�\"�s�K+��� �4Ҧ\u0002�5g�", "jev": {"concrete": 0.24, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.88, "market": "derivatives", "us_long_only": 0.38, "recent": 0.05, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.014}
{"url": "https://www.pm-research.com/content/iijpormgmt/52/1/122.full.pdf", "title": "Better Opt Out? Revisiting the Predictive Power of Options-Implied Signals", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "Johnson and So 2012; An et al. 2014; Fu et al. 2016; Baltussen, van Bekkum, and van der Grient 2018). A common implementation approach involves constructing long−short portfolios based on measures derived from the IV surface. Although historical studies document significant risk-adjusted returns for these strategies, their continued effectiveness and practical implementation challenges warrant closer examination. We revisit and mea sure the performance of a suite of common options-implied signals across the 1996 to 2021 period. We find a stark contrast between market regimes: The Sharpe ratio of a long–short combination trading strategy using several options-implied signals declines from 1.18 in 1996–2008 to 0.16 in 2009–2021, while the annualized alpha falls from 11.40% to 1.56%, losing statistical significance. Exhibit 1 illustrates this combination strategy’s performance over time, highlighting the concentration of returns in the pre-2008 period. The deterioration in signal effectiveness likely stems from multiple sources: adaptation of markets to the publication of signals (McLean and Pontiff 2016), regulatory reforms enhancing market transparency, and previously underappreciated implementation challenges related to timing mismatches between options and stock price data. This timing mismatch, which has significant implications for both signal construction and strategy implementation, is the focal topic of this article. The issue\n...\nand Koijen (2012); and Wallmeier (2024)", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 794, "excerpt": "Clint Howard Iman Honarvar The Journal of Portfolio Management November 2025, 52 ( 1) 122 - 144 DOI: 10.3905/jpm.2025.1.761 Download PDF To download content, you need to upgrade your trial to full subscription. Please contact your account manager to do this. Add to Favorites Please log-in to or register for your personal account in order to save a bookmark. Log-in/register Share Labels Please log-", "jev": {"concrete": 0.18, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.94, "market": "derivatives", "us_long_only": 0.42, "recent": 0.39, "code": 0.03}, "priority": 0.013}
{"url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/empfin/v83y2025ics0927539825000647.html", "title": "Option-implied idiosyncratic skewness and expected returns: Mind the long run", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "This article examines the time-series predictive ability of the monthly option-implied idiosyncratic skewness (Skew) for the aggregate stock market. We find that Skew is a strong predictor of the U.S. equity premium using both in-sample and out-of-sample tests at forecast horizons up to 36 months over the period from January 1996 to December 2021. In comparison, Skew outperforms the previously used financial and macroeconomic variables. Furthermore, combining information in the transitional predictors with Skew can further improve the forecasting performance than using Skew alone. We provide two explanations for the documented predictability. First, Skew exhibits strong procyclical behavior and consistently declines ahead of economic downturns. Second, Skew acts as a forward-looking signal of investor sentiment and disagreement—positive shocks to Skew significantly increase both future investor sentiment and disagreement, with effects that persist over several horizons.\n...\nOption-implied skewness; Predictive regression; Out-of-sample forecast; Combination forecast; All these keywords\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 22804, "excerpt": "Option-implied idiosyncratic skewness and expected returns: Mind the long run Download full text from publisher As the access to this document is restricted, you may want to for a different version of it. References listed on IDEAS - Yu, Deshui & Huang, Difang & Chen, Li, 2023. \"Stock return predictability and cyclical movements in valuation ratios,\" Journal of Empirical Finance, Elsevier, vol. 72", "jev": {"concrete": 0.09, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.93, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.41, "recent": 0.28, "code": 0.31}, "priority": 0.011}
{"url": "https://caepr.indiana.edu/RePEc/inu/caeprp/caepr2025-003.pdf", "title": "Market Returns Dormant in Options", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "power for the future realized P-density. Specifically, we evaluate the null hypotheses that ei ther the past realized P-density or the Q-density has no predictive power for a given moment of the future P-density.1 These hypotheses relate to various predictability patterns in the literature, including implications of the efficient market hypothesis (Fama, 1970), momentum and reversal patterns (Jegadeesh and Titman, 1993; De Bondt and Thaler, 1985; Moskowitz et al., 2012), variance predictability (Engle, 1982; Bollerslev, 1986; Nelson, 1991; Glosten et al., 1993), downside/jump risk clustering (Mandelbrot, 1963; Christie, 1982; Bekaert and Wu, 2000; Andersen et al., 2007), variance risk premium (Carr and Wu, 2009; Bollerslev et al., 2009) and option-implied moments (Bakshi et al., 2003; Chang et al., 2013). Focusing on the predictability of market returns (the first moment of the realized P-density), our tests strongly reject the null hypothesis that the Q-density has no predictive power, confirming its significant forward-looking information for forecasting future returns. In contrast, we fail to reject the null hypothesis for the past realized P-density, suggesting it adds little predic tive power. This framework, akin to a Granger causality test, highlights the central role of option-implied information in forecasting market return distributions. Employing our functional regression framework, we explore the predictive power of risk neutral Q-density functions, drawing on rea", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 914198, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.5 %���� 885 0 obj > endobj 886 0 obj > /W [ 1 3 1 ] /Index [ 885 316 ] /Info 249 0 R /Root 887 0 R /Size 1201 /Prev 1833799 /ID [ ] >> stream x�cbd`�g`b``8 \"�^�Hƽ �u\u0006X|-���\u0006�!Adl\u000f�dY &\u0017�Hf6$�\u0005D��\u0002IF� \u0010{� �ݛ\u0007b'���� �*\u0017#L`Y\u0004\"��$�$�\u0014�g`b���\u0011�\u0006\u0006�Qr����?�n\u0018%�.ɸp��0J\u000eM\u0012\u0000MK\u0016k endstream endobj 887 0 obj > 1 > ] >> /PageMode /UseOutlines /Pages 1054 0 R /Type /Catalog >> endobj 888 0 obj > stream x", "jev": {"concrete": 0.15, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.91, "market": "derivatives", "us_long_only": 0.33, "recent": 0.31, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.009}
{"url": "https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.2023.4872", "title": "Cross-Sectional Variation of Option-Implied Volatility Skew", "engine": "exa", "published": "2023-07-20", "snippet": "# Cross-Sectional Variation of Option-Implied Volatility Skew\n...\nPublished Online:20 Jul 2023 https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2023.4872\n...\nThe stock option-implied volatility skew reflects both the structural risk characteristics of the underlying company and the short-term information flow about the stock price movement. This paper builds a semistructural, cross-sectional option pricing model to separate the structural risk contributions from the information flow. The model identifies two structural risk sources that contribute to the cross-sectional variation of the skew: the company’s business cyclicality and its default risk. The model can explain as much as 44% of the cross-sectional variation in implied volatility skew and is particularly informative during and after recessions. The remaining skew variation reflects mainly short-term information flow and can be used to construct stock portfolios with much better investment performance and without hidden structural risk exposures.\n...\n- implied volatility skew\n- risk-neutral return skewness\n- cyclicality\n- default risk\n- structural risk exposures\n- information flow\n- stock return prediction\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "jina", "text_chars": 27939, "excerpt": "Title: Cross-Sectional Variation of Option-Implied Volatility Skew URL Source: https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mnsc.2023.4872 Markdown Content: This website stores cookies on your computer. These cookies are used to collect information about how you interact with our website and allow us to remember you. We use this information in order to improve and customize your browsing experience ", "jev": {"concrete": 0.06, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 1.0, "market": "derivatives", "us_long_only": 0.34, "recent": 0.39, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.004}
